<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhang Qian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 113 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhang Qian was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> envoy sent west by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> around 138 BCE to seek an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. Held captive by the Xiongnu for years, he reached Dayuan, Kangju and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and returned in 126 BCE; a later mission went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wusun">Wusun</a>. His reports gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> its first detailed knowledge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and opened the way for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhang-qian.jpg" alt="Zhang Qian" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHG · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZhangQianTravels.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhang Qian was born in Chenggu district, just east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanzhong">Hanzhong</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaanxi">Shaanxi</a>. He entered the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, between 140 and 134 BCE as a Gentleman serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>. At that time the nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> controlled what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a> and dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a>, the lands neighbouring Han territory, and their hostility blocked outside contact. The emperor wished to establish commercial ties with distant lands and to find allies against the Xiongnu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 138 BCE the Han court sent Zhang Qian, a military officer familiar with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, with a party of ninety-nine to seek an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a>. His guide was a captured Xiongnu named Ganfu. The Xiongnu seized them on the way and held Zhang for thirteen years, during which he married a Xiongnu wife and had a son. When he escaped he went by Dayuan to the Yuezhi, who were too settled to wish to fight the Xiongnu. He spent a year among them and in neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, recording their customs and economy. On the return he was captured again, and escaped in the disorder after the Xiongnu leader died. Of just over a hundred men, only he and Ganfu returned.</p><p>He brought the emperor detailed news of sophisticated states to the west, which according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> were rich in unusual products and militarily weak. He was made a palace counsellor. A later mission sought a route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and failed, and another, between 119 and 115 BCE, went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wusun">Wusun</a>. In 121 BCE he took part in a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, but he lost his way and failed to meet the general Li Guang, whose force was surrounded and defeated. Zhang was sentenced to death and escaped by paying a ransom, which cost him his titles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Zhang Qian's reports are quoted extensively by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a>. He visited Dayuan in Fergana, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Oxus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and Kangju, and reported on lands he had not seen, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. He described the horses of Dayuan, which led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a> to launch two campaigns to obtain them, and brought back alfalfa seeds for fodder. The Shiji says he was honoured with the post of grand messenger after his final expedition and died a year or so later.</p><p>After his embassy, Chinese missions went west in numbers, and the largest numbered several hundred people. Because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> became the main product traded from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the route later became known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, although exchanges along parts of it had occurred before Zhang Qian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhang Qian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 126 BCE the Han envoy <b>Zhang Qian</b> found a population of 1,000,000 or more, people who farmed, lived in cities and were clever at commerce, with a great market at the capital, Lanshi (Bactra), where he saw bamboo from Qiong and cloth from Shu bought in India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It was opened by <b>Zhang Qian</b>'s missions and Emperor Wu's western campaigns and sustained by Han military presence across the Tarim Basin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/han-dynasty">Han Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Zhang Qian</b>, visiting Bactria about 128 BCE, found bamboo canes from Qiong and cloth from Shu in its markets and was told that merchants bought them in Shendu, northwestern India, a settled farming land like Bactria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its position along the Silk Road allowed goods, ideas and technologies to move between the Roman world and Han China, which first established contact with Parthia through embassies following the mission of <b>Zhang Qian</b> in the second century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Han search for allies against the Xiongnu also sent <b>Zhang Qian</b> on his celebrated mission to the Yuezhi in 139 BCE, which opened Chinese knowledge of Central Asia and helped to establish the routes later known as the Silk Road.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the Shiji of Sima Qian, which draws on the report of the envoy <b>Zhang Qian</b>, they originally lived between Dunhuang and the Qilian Mountains in the Gansu corridor, and in the late third century BCE they were strong enough to look down on the Xiongnu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chenggu County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in China. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.15,107.33&amp;year=-123" aria-label="Chenggu County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7297 960 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Zhang Qian"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7747 1230h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7747 1230h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.15,107.33&amp;year=-123">What was here in 123 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
